{"id":"271386574a422140c55942a5a345ede858afb5cc","axis":"silent","repo":"apache/spark","sha":"271386574a422140c55942a5a345ede858afb5cc","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/sql-migration-guide.md","lines":["@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@ license: |"," "," ## Upgrading from Spark SQL 4.3 to 4.4"," ","+- Since Spark 4.4, quoted Variant path keys decode `\\\\`, `\\/`, `\\b`, `\\f`, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\uXXXX` (including valid surrogate pairs), and escapes of the enclosing quote (`\\'` or `\\\"`). Previously, backslashes were literal, so existing paths can match different keys or become invalid. For example, use `$['a\\\\']` instead of `$['a\\']`. Unknown escapes and unpaired surrogate escapes stay literal. Dot-notation keys are unchanged. To keep the old behavior, set `spark.sql.legacy.variantPathBackslashAsLiteral` to `true` before creating the query."," - Since Spark 4.4, `spark.sql.execution.arrow.pythonUDF.columnarInput.enabled` is `false` by default: Arrow-based Python UDFs read rows from an Arrow-backed upstream operator, such as a Data Source V2 scan or the Arrow cache, as before Spark 4.2. With columnar input, they kept the columns that they pass through until the results of their batch came back from Python, and failed or returned wrong results when the operator reused or released the vectors of the batch in the meantime. To enable columnar input again, set `spark.sql.execution.arrow.pythonUDF.columnarInput.enabled` to `true`."," - Since Spark 4.4, `spark.sql.charVarchar.standardSemantics.enabled` (default `false`) is a preview feature that may change in future releases. When true, it enables first-class `CHAR(n)` and `VARCHAR(n)` semantics throughout schemas and query plans: `CHAR` -> `VARCHAR` -> `STRING` form a type-precedence family, explicit casts can return constrained types, least-common-type resolution for `COALESCE`/`CASE`/`UNION` can return `CHAR` or `VARCHAR`, and string expressions that transform their input return `STRING`. Assignment and reads enforce the declared length, padding `CHAR` to `n` characters and raising `EXCEED_LIMIT_LENGTH` when non-space content does not fit. Comparing a CHAR column with a string literal no longer pads the literal, so `WHERE char_col = 'ab'` does not match a stored `'ab  '`. The default remains the Spark 3.1 table-schema overlay on `STRING`. This opt-in mode is preferred over the experimental `spark.sql.preserveCharVarcharTypeInfo`, which instead keeps constrained types through transforming expressions. See [Character String Types](sql-ref-character-string-types.html) for the preview semantics; the entries below note area-specific behavior that changed. Later CHAR/VARCHAR bullets that do not name `preserveCharVarcharTypeInfo` apply when the preview flag is true, except `from_avro` value assignment, which also runs when only `preserveCharVarcharTypeInfo` is true."," - Since Spark 4.4, when `spark.sql.charVarchar.standardSemantics.enabled` is true, `from_json` length-checks JSON object names used as `MAP<CHAR(n), _>` or `MAP<VARCHAR(n), _>` keys without padding or trimming them. A `CHAR(n)` key must already be exactly `n` characters, and a `VARCHAR(n)` key must already be at most `n` characters; duplicate names are kept, exactly as for STRING keys, and `spark.sql.mapKeyDedupPolicy` is not applied. A key that fails the check makes the enclosing map a bad record: in `PERMISSIVE` mode the nearest enclosing struct field becomes `null` (its sibling fields are preserved), and if the map is inside an array or another map, that whole array or map becomes `null`; in `FAILFAST` mode parsing fails with `UNSUPPORTED_JSON_CHAR_VARCHAR_MAP_KEY` (SQLSTATE `0A000`) as the cause of `MALFORMED_RECORD_IN_PARSING`. The same parser-level check runs for any JSON scan whose reader schema carries a CHAR/VARCHAR map key, including catalog JSON tables, since under standard semantics those keys stay CHAR/VARCHAR all the way to the scan: an off-width key becomes a bad record there too, rather than being padded or raising `EXCEED_LIMIT_LENGTH`. Unlike `from_json`, keys that pass the check are then rebuilt read-side, so on the scan path `spark.sql.mapKeyDedupPolicy` does apply and a duplicate name can raise `DUPLICATED_MAP_KEY`. JSON file scans, including scans inside persisted views, read the flag from the executing session. The optimizer no longer folds `from_json(to_json(m))` to `m` when that schema has a CHAR/VARCHAR map key, so the length check still runs on the round trip."],"additions":1,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"sql/catalyst/src/main/java/org/apache/spark/sql/connector/expressions/VariantGet.java","lines":["@@ -44,7 +44,9 @@ public class VariantGet extends ExpressionWithToString {","   /**","    * Creates VariantGet expression.","    * @param child variant column reference","-   * @param path JSON path string","+   * @param path extraction path using Spark's default Variant path grammar. See","+   *             {@link org.apache.spark.sql.connector.read.VariantExtraction#metadata()} with","+   *             {@code legacyPathBackslashAsLiteral=false} for bracket-key escape rules.","    * @param targetType expected result type","    * @param failOnError whether to throw on cast failure ({@code variant_get}) or return null","    *                    ({@code try_variant_get})"],"additions":3,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"sql/catalyst/src/main/java/org/apache/spark/sql/connector/read/VariantExtraction.java","lines":["@@ -58,6 +58,9 @@ public interface VariantExtraction extends Serializable {","    * - \"failOnError\": whether the extraction to expected data type should throw an exception","    *                  or return null if the cast fails.","    * - \"timeZoneId\": a string identifier of a time zone. It is required by timestamp-related casts.","+   * - \"legacyPathBackslashAsLiteral\": whether backslashes in bracket-quoted keys are literal.","+   *   If false, decodes \\\\, \\/, \\b, \\f, \\n, \\r, \\t, Unicode and enclosing-quote escapes.","+  